## Method
Conda environment: gars-bio (default).

1. Load the gene count matrix from the resolved TSV file using pandas. Verify dimensions (genes × samples).

2. Filter genes to retain only those with count-per-million (CPM) ≥ 1 in at least 3 samples (half the smallest group size). This removes genes with negligible expression.

3. Apply counts-per-million (CPM) normalization: divide each sample column by its sum and multiply by 1e6 to account for library size differences.

4. Log-transform normalized counts: add pseudocount of 1, then apply natural logarithm. This stabilizes variance across the expression range.

5. Center and scale the log-transformed counts to zero mean and unit variance per gene using scikit-learn's StandardScaler.

6. Perform PCA using scikit-learn's PCA function. Retain components explaining 95% of variance. Compute loadings (PC×gene) and scores (sample×PC).

7. Create a bivariate scatter plot of the first two principal components (PC1 vs PC2), colored by condition (untreated = blue, treated = red). Add ellipses to denote sample groups. Label axes with % variance explained. Create using matplotlib/seaborn.

8. Create a bar plot showing cumulative variance explained by successive principal components (up to 20 PCs).

9. Write all results to CSV and PNG files in results/ directory (see Outputs section).

